    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7516",
      "problem": "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Find the graph of $y=-f(x+3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/8c57e0ebec3c6dcc4b6d1b72a8dde395fbe4a90801c18718fc36edf84469af4f.png)",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $3$ units $y=f(x+3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/0770e092f1d63c0a9c4416f92fa6e543ffcc47bcd78375f5b835b64df3ef691a.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the  $x$- axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x+3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/5fde1d3543593322ad5caebbf28d4623571032e35c516dd051583294f452e679.png)",
